(*  Title:       Dual_Write_Effect_Cursor.thy
    Author:      Andreas Andreakis
    SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause

    THE SAME-PROTOCOL CHECKPOINT DILEMMA: the cursor-bearing effect
    machine --- an additive post-freeze slice under the freeze's own
    protocol: no landed statement, proof, definition, name, or import is
    changed; the only touched file outside this one is the session ROOT,
    which gains this theory.

    WHAT THIS THEORY ANSWERS.  The landed headline dilemma
    (Dual_Write_Effect_Dilemma) defeats every store-measured and
    (core, epoch)-measured re-drive policy on a designed equal-core pair
    whose two members arise from the SAME crashed state through two
    DIFFERENT values of the reconcile's cursor parameter m --- an
    information bound whose pair is exhibited by externally chosen
    reconcile indices.  The standing external objection: a real
    implementation does not receive m from outside --- it carries its own
    durable progress cursor, and the two confusable histories should be
    generated by ONE fixed deterministic implementation, with crash
    timing as the only nondeterminism.  This theory proves exactly that
    PROTOCOL-grade bound.

    THE MACHINE (prefix dwk, for the checkpointed machine; dwc is the
    channel machine's prefix).  dwk_state wraps the canonical CYCLIC
    effect machine state (Dual_Write_Effect_Cyclic --- not the terminal
    twin) with one nat field: a durable progress cursor.  Its four
    transition kinds: (i) every cyclic label step lifts with the cursor
    unchanged --- in particular the sink delivery DoDownstream; (ii) a
    NEW cursor-persist transition advances the cursor by one, guarded by
    an actual delivery to acknowledge (cursor < |down_hist|, Running) ---
    delivery and persistence are SEPARATE transitions, so a crash is
    admissible between them; (iii) the machine's only recovery transition
    is the cyclic emitting reconcile with the index m INSTANTIATED BY THE
    MACHINE'S OWN CURSOR --- the action DWK_Reconcile carries only the
    frontier: the free m of the audited abstraction is closed by its
    durable realization (cursor_realizes_reconcile_index,
    machine_recovery_is_cursor_policy); (iv) Resume lifts unchanged.
    The wrapper is conservative: every dwk trace projects to a cyclic
    trace (dwk_trace_base_projects; the persist step is a cyclic
    stutter), so every dwk-reachable state has a cyclic-reachable base
    and inherits the banked invariants (dwk_reachable_stamps_bounded).

    THE PROTOCOL.  protocol_next is a FUNCTION from states to optional
    actions --- the faithful deliver-then-persist discipline over the
    pinned two-event workload: commit e1, enqueue e1, deliver e1, persist;
    commit e2, enqueue e2, deliver e2, persist; stop.  A protocol run
    consumes a crash SCHEDULE over the two-letter alphabet
    {Sched_Go, Sched_Crash}: Go executes the protocol's unique next
    action, Crash is the adversary's move (the machine's own status-flip
    crash, its frontier tag pinned to the finish frontier ec2 --- the
    adversary chooses WHEN, never a parameter).  The protocol itself
    never crashes (protocol_never_crashes), and the endpoint of a run is
    a FUNCTION of the schedule (protocol_run_deterministic): crash
    placement is the only nondeterminism, and no per-run parameter
    exists to distinguish runs.

    THE PAIR.  Schedules sched_deliver = Go^7 Crash and
    sched_lag = Go^6 Crash differ only in where the crash lands: after
    the second delivery but before its persist, versus before the second
    delivery.  Both runs end crashed with cursor 1 --- the delivered
    member inside the second deliver-to-persist window, the lag member
    before that window opens --- and the two endpoints agree on the
    ENTIRE durable-local view (below), the cursor included, while their
    emission ledgers differ: [(1,0,ec1,e1),(2,0,ec2,e2)] versus
    [(1,0,ec1,e1)].  The lag member's base is literally the banked
    loaded-window crash state t_mid_w.

    THE DESIGN PREAMBLE (the durable projection, the one substantive
    modelling decision --- read this before the theorems).  The
    durable-local view (durable_local_view) is what a recovery procedure
    of the crashed consumer reads WITHOUT querying the sink side: the
    base map, the committed source WAL, the enqueue (outbox) log, the
    ack log, the scope, the finish frontier, the run status, the
    recovery epoch, and the progress cursor.  Three components of the
    full state are deliberately OUTSIDE it: the emission ledger (the
    external world's record of received effects --- the sink's ledger,
    per the landed two-store reading), the downstream store content
    exec_down_hist (the delivered material itself: reading it IS reading
    the sink --- the content-idempotent downstream reading --- and is
    proved here to sit on the escape side, content_delta_separates_pair),
    and the in-flight pending window (volatile as this theory's own
    modelling premise: the crashed process's in-memory delivery buffer,
    which the landed reconcile itself always discards; the landed
    core-tier truncation theory Dual_Write_Crash_Truncation does not
    lose it but reconstructs it at a durable boundary as durable
    enqueued minus durable delivered --- a route that reads the excluded
    downstream content; pending_probe_separates_pair prices this
    exclusion).  The crash
    itself is UNCHANGED cyclic physics --- a status flip that keeps every
    field; the carve is epistemic, in the policy class, never physical.

    WHY THE CARVE IS FORCED, NOT CHOSEN: both runs are Resume-free, so
    their crashed bases are epoch-0 cyclic-reachable states, and the
    landed pre-first-Resume negative (Dual_Write_Effect_Cycle_Necessity:
    before the first Resume no reachable pair of crashed states has
    equal cores and payload-divergent ledgers) makes an EQUAL-CORE
    protocol pair impossible at epoch 0.  The pair here is equal-VIEW
    and core-divergent (pair_cores_differ discloses it: the delivered
    member's down_hist and pending record the delivery) --- a different
    certificate shape, fully consistent with the landed negative.

    THE HEADLINE (house shape and polarity: exhibit the pair, then
    defeat the class).  window_pair_batch_agreement_defeated exports the
    kernel: every policy emitting the same batch on the two members is
    defeated --- its own redrive heals every scoped store mismatch on
    both, yet duplicates or goes premature on the delivered member or
    loses the committed second effect on the lag member.
    same_protocol_batch_agreement_dilemma packages the pair with its two
    protocol runs inline; checkpoint_dilemma_pair / checkpoint_dilemma
    defeat every durable_local_measured policy --- every recovery whose
    batch is a function of the durable-local view, the cursor INCLUDED.
    The machine's own faithful recovery is the inhabitation witness
    (cursor_resume_policy_durable_local_measured) and its two horns are
    realized concretely (faithful_recovery_window_horns): the SAME
    parameter-free recovery transition, fired from the two
    view-indistinguishable members, is exactly right on the lag member
    and an effect-unsafe duplicate on the delivered member --- the
    at-least-once window made a theorem.

    THE DICHOTOMY BRIDGE.  The landed sink-reading escape distinguishes
    the pair (sink_delta_separates_pair), is provably not
    durable-local-measured, and --- cited from the landed premise-free
    general form --- heals, stays effect-safe, and loses nothing from
    BOTH members (sink_delta_escapes_at_pair).  The durable cursor does
    not rescue ledger-blindness; only reading the sink does, now at
    protocol grade.  checkpoint_recovery_dichotomy is the literally
    exhaustive split: every policy either factors through the
    durable-local view (and the pair defeats it) or distinguishes two
    states agreeing on that entire view --- i.e. reads the downstream
    content, the volatile window, or the emission ledger.

    THE WINDOW CONTROL.  post_persist_cursor_separates: crashing AFTER
    the final persist yields cursor 2, and the cursor alone separates
    that state's view from both pair members --- outside the
    deliver-to-persist window the cursor tells the truth; the dilemma
    is the window, not the cursor's bookkeeping.

    Exists-system polarity throughout: every defeat lives at the
    designed pair of the pinned witness machine; no for-every-system
    claim, no minimality claim, and nothing new is claimed about the
    landed theorems.  All witnesses are closed states with computed
    verdicts, in the landed witness/regression style.
*)

theory Dual_Write_Effect_Cursor
  imports Dual_Write_Effect_Dilemma
begin

section \<open>The cursor-bearing machine: state, actions, moves, traces\<close>

text \<open>
  The wrapper state is the cyclic machine state plus one durable nat:
  the progress cursor.  @{text dwk} abbreviates the checkpointed
  (cursor-bearing) machine; the @{text dwc} prefix is already taken by
  the channel machine of the fencing branch.
\<close>

record ('k, 'v) dwk_state =
  dwk_base   :: "('k, 'v) dwe_state"
  dwk_cursor :: nat

definition dwk_init
  :: "('k \<rightharpoonup> 'v) \<Rightarrow> 'k set \<Rightarrow> frontier \<Rightarrow> ('k, 'v) dwk_state"
where
  "dwk_init b K fin = \<lparr> dwk_base = dwe_init b K fin, dwk_cursor = 0 \<rparr>"

lemma dwk_init_base [simp]: "dwk_base (dwk_init b K fin) = dwe_init b K fin"
  by (simp add: dwk_init_def)

lemma dwk_init_cursor [simp]: "dwk_cursor (dwk_init b K fin) = 0"
  by (simp add: dwk_init_def)

text \<open>
  Actions.  @{text DWK_Reconcile} carries ONLY the frontier: the index
  @{term m} that the cyclic action @{const DWE_Reconcile} carries as an
  action parameter is not an input of this machine --- its recovery rule
  below instantiates @{term m} with the state's own durable cursor, the
  durable realization of what the cyclic machine abstracts as a
  parameter.  This signature is the formal answer to the
  externally-chosen-index objection.
\<close>

datatype ('k, 'v) dwk_act =
    DWK_Label "('k, 'v) dw_exec_label"
  | DWK_Persist
  | DWK_Reconcile frontier
  | DWK_Resume

text \<open>
  Single moves.  The label rule carries the same wellformedness guards
  as the cyclic temporal trace, so dwk traces project to cyclic traces
  rule by rule.  The persist rule is the durable checkpoint write:
  Running only (a dead process writes no checkpoints), and the cursor
  may only advance to acknowledge an actual delivery
  (@{term "dwk_cursor T < length (exec_down_hist (dwe_core (dwk_base T)))"});
  the RUNNING implementation reads its own live state freely --- the
  epistemic restriction of this theory is on post-crash RECOVERY, never
  on the live protocol.  The reconcile rule is the machine's only
  recovery transition: the cyclic emitting reconcile at
  @{term "m = dwk_cursor T"}; its in-range guard
  (@{term "m \<le> length R"}) is part of the landed
  @{const emitting_reconcile} and is inherited unchanged.
\<close>

inductive dwk_move
  :: "('k, 'v) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> ('k, 'v) dwk_act \<Rightarrow> ('k, 'v) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> bool"
where
  move_label:
    "wellformed_exec_state (dwe_core (dwk_base T)) \<Longrightarrow>
     exec_label_preserves_history_wf (dwe_core (dwk_base T)) a \<Longrightarrow>
     dwe_step (dwk_base T) a B' \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_move T (DWK_Label a) (T\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>)"
| move_persist:
    "exec_status (dwe_core (dwk_base T)) = Running \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_cursor T < length (exec_down_hist (dwe_core (dwk_base T))) \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_move T DWK_Persist (T\<lparr>dwk_cursor := Suc (dwk_cursor T)\<rparr>)"
| move_reconcile:
    "emitting_reconcile (dwk_cursor T) (dwk_base T) f B' \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_move T (DWK_Reconcile f) (T\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>)"
| move_resume:
    "dwe_resume (dwk_base T) B' \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_move T DWK_Resume (T\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>)"

inductive_cases dwk_move_labelE: "dwk_move T (DWK_Label a) T'"
inductive_cases dwk_move_persistE: "dwk_move T DWK_Persist T'"
inductive_cases dwk_move_reconcileE: "dwk_move T (DWK_Reconcile f) T'"
inductive_cases dwk_move_resumeE: "dwk_move T DWK_Resume T'"

text \<open>Witness-state builder and its selector equations, in the landed
  @{const mkT} / @{const mkC} style.\<close>

definition mkW :: "('k, 'v) dwe_state \<Rightarrow> nat \<Rightarrow> ('k, 'v) dwk_state" where
  "mkW B n = \<lparr> dwk_base = B, dwk_cursor = n \<rparr>"

lemma mkW_base [simp]: "dwk_base (mkW B n) = B"
  by (simp add: mkW_def)

lemma mkW_cursor [simp]: "dwk_cursor (mkW B n) = n"
  by (simp add: mkW_def)

text \<open>Derived introduction rules at @{const mkW} states (for the
  closed-witness chains).\<close>

lemma mkW_move_labelI:
  assumes "wellformed_exec_state (dwe_core B)"
      and "exec_label_preserves_history_wf (dwe_core B) a"
      and "dwe_step B a B'"
  shows "dwk_move (mkW B n) (DWK_Label a) (mkW B' n)"
proof -
  have "dwk_move (mkW B n) (DWK_Label a) ((mkW B n)\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>)"
    by (rule dwk_move.move_label) (simp_all add: assms)
  then show ?thesis by (simp add: mkW_def)
qed

lemma mkW_move_persistI:
  assumes "exec_status (dwe_core B) = Running"
      and "n < length (exec_down_hist (dwe_core B))"
  shows "dwk_move (mkW B n) DWK_Persist (mkW B (Suc n))"
proof -
  have "dwk_move (mkW B n) DWK_Persist
          ((mkW B n)\<lparr>dwk_cursor := Suc (dwk_cursor (mkW B n))\<rparr>)"
    by (rule dwk_move.move_persist) (simp_all add: assms)
  then show ?thesis by (simp add: mkW_def)
qed

lemma mkW_move_reconcileI:
  assumes "emitting_reconcile n B f B'"
  shows "dwk_move (mkW B n) (DWK_Reconcile f) (mkW B' n)"
proof -
  have "dwk_move (mkW B n) (DWK_Reconcile f) ((mkW B n)\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>)"
    by (rule dwk_move.move_reconcile) (simp_all add: assms)
  then show ?thesis by (simp add: mkW_def)
qed

lemma mkW_move_resumeI:
  assumes "dwe_resume B B'"
  shows "dwk_move (mkW B n) DWK_Resume (mkW B' n)"
proof -
  have "dwk_move (mkW B n) DWK_Resume ((mkW B n)\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>)"
    by (rule dwk_move.move_resume) (simp_all add: assms)
  then show ?thesis by (simp add: mkW_def)
qed

text \<open>Traces and pinned reachability, mirroring the cyclic machine.\<close>

inductive dwk_trace
  :: "('k, 'v) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> ('k, 'v) dwk_act list \<Rightarrow> ('k, 'v) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> bool"
where
  dwk_refl: "dwk_trace T [] T"
| dwk_cons: "dwk_move T x T' \<Longrightarrow> dwk_trace T' xs T'' \<Longrightarrow>
             dwk_trace T (x # xs) T''"

lemma dwk_trace_append:
  assumes "dwk_trace T xs T'"
      and "dwk_trace T' ys T''"
  shows "dwk_trace T (xs @ ys) T''"
  using assms
  by (induction rule: dwk_trace.induct) (auto intro: dwk_trace.intros)

definition dwk_reachable :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> bool" where
  "dwk_reachable T \<longleftrightarrow>
     (\<exists>xs. dwk_trace (dwk_init Map.empty {0, 1} ec2) xs T)"

lemma dwk_reachable_init: "dwk_reachable (dwk_init Map.empty {0, 1} ec2)"
  unfolding dwk_reachable_def by (blast intro: dwk_trace.dwk_refl)

lemma dwk_reachable_trace_ext:
  assumes "dwk_reachable T"
      and "dwk_trace T xs T'"
  shows "dwk_reachable T'"
  using assms unfolding dwk_reachable_def
  by (blast intro: dwk_trace_append)

section \<open>Conservativity: every dwk run projects to a cyclic run\<close>

text \<open>
  A label move projects to the cyclic label step, the reconcile move to
  the cyclic reconcile at the run's own cursor value, Resume to Resume,
  and the persist move to the EMPTY cyclic trace (a cyclic stutter: it
  touches no base field).  So the cursor machine adds bookkeeping, never
  behaviour, over the cyclic machine --- the wrap is conservative, and
  every dwk-reachable base is cyclic-reachable.
\<close>

lemma dwk_move_base_projects:
  assumes "dwk_move T x T'"
  shows "\<exists>as. dwe_temporal_trace (dwk_base T) as (dwk_base T')"
  using assms
proof (cases rule: dwk_move.cases)
  case (move_label a B')
  then have "dwe_temporal_trace (dwk_base T) [DWE_Label a] B'"
    by (blast intro: dwe_temporal_trace.dwe_label_step
                     dwe_temporal_trace.dwe_refl)
  with move_label show ?thesis by auto
next
  case move_persist
  then show ?thesis
    by (auto intro: dwe_temporal_trace.dwe_refl)
next
  case (move_reconcile f B')
  then have "dwe_temporal_trace (dwk_base T)
               [DWE_Reconcile (dwk_cursor T) f] B'"
    by (blast intro: dwe_temporal_trace.dwe_reconcile_step
                     dwe_temporal_trace.dwe_refl)
  with move_reconcile show ?thesis by auto
next
  case (move_resume B')
  then have "dwe_temporal_trace (dwk_base T) [DWE_Resume] B'"
    by (blast intro: dwe_temporal_trace.dwe_resume_step
                     dwe_temporal_trace.dwe_refl)
  with move_resume show ?thesis by auto
qed

theorem dwk_trace_base_projects:
  assumes "dwk_trace T xs T'"
  shows "\<exists>as. dwe_temporal_trace (dwk_base T) as (dwk_base T')"
  using assms
proof (induction rule: dwk_trace.induct)
  case (dwk_refl T)
  then show ?case by (blast intro: dwe_temporal_trace.dwe_refl)
next
  case (dwk_cons T x T' xs T'')
  obtain as1 where a1: "dwe_temporal_trace (dwk_base T) as1 (dwk_base T')"
    using dwk_move_base_projects[OF dwk_cons.hyps(1)] by blast
  obtain as2 where a2: "dwe_temporal_trace (dwk_base T') as2 (dwk_base T'')"
    using dwk_cons.IH by blast
  show ?case using dwe_temporal_trace_append[OF a1 a2] by blast
qed

corollary dwk_reachable_base_cyclic:
  assumes "dwk_reachable T"
  shows "dwe_reachable (dwk_base T)"
proof -
  obtain xs where "dwk_trace (dwk_init Map.empty {0, 1} ec2) xs T"
    using assms unfolding dwk_reachable_def by blast
  from dwk_trace_base_projects[OF this] show ?thesis
    unfolding dwe_reachable_def by simp
qed

text \<open>The conservativity payoff: banked cyclic invariants transfer to
  every reachable base --- e.g. the load-bearing stamp bound.\<close>

corollary dwk_reachable_stamps_bounded:
  assumes "dwk_reachable T"
  shows "stamps_bounded (dwk_base T)"
  by (rule dwe_reachable_stamps_bounded[OF dwk_reachable_base_cyclic[OF assms]])

section \<open>The cursor realizes the reconcile index m\<close>

text \<open>
  The bridging facts promised by the design preamble.  At transition
  level: the machine's recovery transition is EXACTLY the cyclic
  emitting reconcile with the abstract index @{term m} instantiated by
  the state's own durable cursor --- @{term m} is no longer an input.
  (The policy-level bridge, @{text machine_recovery_is_cursor_policy},
  follows in the recovery section once the policy vocabulary is set up.)
\<close>

theorem cursor_realizes_reconcile_index:
  "dwk_move T (DWK_Reconcile f) T' \<longleftrightarrow>
     (\<exists>B'. emitting_reconcile (dwk_cursor T) (dwk_base T) f B'
         \<and> T' = T\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>)"
proof
  assume "dwk_move T (DWK_Reconcile f) T'"
  then show "\<exists>B'. emitting_reconcile (dwk_cursor T) (dwk_base T) f B'
                \<and> T' = T\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>"
    by (cases rule: dwk_move.cases) auto
next
  assume "\<exists>B'. emitting_reconcile (dwk_cursor T) (dwk_base T) f B'
             \<and> T' = T\<lparr>dwk_base := B'\<rparr>"
  then show "dwk_move T (DWK_Reconcile f) T'"
    by (blast intro: dwk_move.move_reconcile)
qed

section \<open>Determinism infrastructure\<close>

text \<open>
  The cyclic label step is a function of (state, label): the landed core
  determinism (@{thm [source] dw_exec_step_deterministic}) lifts through
  both wrapper rules.  Every dwk move is then a function of
  (state, action), and --- the fact the protocol section consumes --- a
  protocol run's endpoint is a function of its crash schedule alone.
\<close>

lemma dwe_step_deterministic:
  assumes "dwe_step T a U"
      and "dwe_step T a V"
  shows "U = V"
  using assms
  by (auto elim!: dwe_step.cases dest: dw_exec_step_deterministic)

lemma dwk_move_deterministic:
  assumes "dwk_move T x U"
      and "dwk_move T x V"
  shows "U = V"
  using assms
  by (auto elim!: dwk_move.cases
           simp: emitting_reconcile_def dwe_resume_def
           dest: dwe_step_deterministic)

section \<open>The deliver-then-persist protocol and its crash scheduler\<close>

text \<open>
  The protocol is a FUNCTION.  Its program counter is reconstructed from
  the state --- along the pinned two-event workload the sum of the four
  progress measures advances by exactly one per protocol step, from 0 at
  the initial state to 8 at completion.  The dispatcher prescribes the
  unique next action at each counter value; it is total as a function,
  and off the protocol's own orbit its prescription may simply fail to
  fire (the run relation below requires the actual machine move).  The
  live protocol reads its own full state --- including the delivery
  count --- exactly as a running implementation reads its memory; the
  durable-local restriction of this theory applies to post-crash
  recovery, never to the live process.
\<close>

definition protocol_pc :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> nat" where
  "protocol_pc T =
     length (exec_src_hist (dwe_core (dwk_base T)))
   + length (exec_enqueued (dwe_core (dwk_base T)))
   + length (exec_down_hist (dwe_core (dwk_base T)))
   + dwk_cursor T"

definition protocol_next
  :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> (nat, nat) dwk_act option"
where
  "protocol_next T =
    (if exec_status (dwe_core (dwk_base T)) \<noteq> Running then None
     else if protocol_pc T = 0 then Some (DWK_Label (DoSource ec1 e1))
     else if protocol_pc T = 1 then Some (DWK_Label (EnqueueDownstream ec1 e1))
     else if protocol_pc T = 2 then Some (DWK_Label (DoDownstream ec1 e1))
     else if protocol_pc T = 3 then Some DWK_Persist
     else if protocol_pc T = 4 then Some (DWK_Label (DoSource ec2 e2))
     else if protocol_pc T = 5 then Some (DWK_Label (EnqueueDownstream ec2 e2))
     else if protocol_pc T = 6 then Some (DWK_Label (DoDownstream ec2 e2))
     else if protocol_pc T = 7 then Some DWK_Persist
     else None)"

text \<open>The protocol never crashes itself: crashes belong to the
  adversary alone.\<close>

lemma protocol_never_crashes:
  assumes "protocol_next T = Some x"
  shows "\<forall>c. x \<noteq> DWK_Label (Crash c)"
  using assms
  by (auto simp: protocol_next_def split: if_split_asm)

text \<open>
  Protocol runs.  The adversary's alphabet has two letters: let the
  protocol execute its unique next action, or crash NOW.  The crash is
  the machine's own lifted status-flip crash; its frontier tag is pinned
  to the finish frontier @{const ec2} (a bookkeeping tag of the landed
  crash label, fixed across all runs --- the adversary chooses only the
  WHEN).  A protocol run is by construction a machine run
  (@{text protocol_run_is_machine_trace}).
\<close>

datatype dwk_sched = Sched_Go | Sched_Crash

inductive dwk_protocol_run
  :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> dwk_sched list \<Rightarrow> (nat, nat) dwk_state \<Rightarrow> bool"
where
  run_refl:
    "dwk_protocol_run T [] T"
| run_go:
    "protocol_next T = Some x \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_move T x T' \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_protocol_run T' ms T'' \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_protocol_run T (Sched_Go # ms) T''"
| run_crash:
    "dwk_move T (DWK_Label (Crash ec2)) T' \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_protocol_run T' ms T'' \<Longrightarrow>
     dwk_protocol_run T (Sched_Crash # ms) T''"

inductive_cases dwk_run_NilE: "dwk_protocol_run T [] U"
inductive_cases dwk_run_GoE: "dwk_protocol_run T (Sched_Go # ms) U"
inductive_cases dwk_run_CrashE: "dwk_protocol_run T (Sched_Crash # ms) U"

lemma protocol_run_is_machine_trace:
  assumes "dwk_protocol_run T ms T'"
  shows "\<exists>xs. dwk_trace T xs T'"
  using assms
proof (induction rule: dwk_protocol_run.induct)
  case (run_refl T)
  then show ?case by (blast intro: dwk_trace.dwk_refl)
next
  case (run_go T x T' ms T'')
  then show ?case by (blast intro: dwk_trace.dwk_cons)
next
  case (run_crash T T' ms T'')
  then show ?case by (blast intro: dwk_trace.dwk_cons)
qed

lemma protocol_run_reachable:
  assumes "dwk_protocol_run (dwk_init Map.empty {0, 1} ec2) ms T"
  shows "dwk_reachable T"
  using protocol_run_is_machine_trace[OF assms]
  unfolding dwk_reachable_def by blast

text \<open>
  THE DETERMINISM THEOREM: the endpoint of a protocol run is a function
  of the crash schedule.  The protocol has no choices
  (@{const protocol_next} is a function), the crash has no parameters,
  and every machine move is deterministic --- so crash PLACEMENT is the
  only nondeterminism, and no externally chosen per-run parameter can
  distinguish two runs.
\<close>

theorem protocol_run_deterministic:
  assumes "dwk_protocol_run T ms U"
      and "dwk_protocol_run T ms V"
  shows "U = V"
  using assms
proof (induction arbitrary: V rule: dwk_protocol_run.induct)
  case (run_refl T)
  from run_refl.prems show ?case
    by (auto elim: dwk_run_NilE)
next
  case (run_go T x T' ms T'')
  from run_go.prems obtain x2 T2 where
      a: "protocol_next T = Some x2" and b: "dwk_move T x2 T2"
      and c: "dwk_protocol_run T2 ms V"
    by (blast elim: dwk_run_GoE)
  have "x2 = x" using run_go.hyps(1) a by simp
  then have "T2 = T'"
    using dwk_move_deterministic[OF run_go.hyps(2)] b by simp
  then show ?case using run_go.IH c by simp
next
  case (run_crash T T' ms T'')
  from run_crash.prems obtain T2 where
      b: "dwk_move T (DWK_Label (Crash ec2)) T2"
      and c: "dwk_protocol_run T2 ms V"
    by (blast elim: dwk_run_CrashE)
  have "T2 = T'"
    using dwk_move_deterministic[OF run_crash.hyps(1) b] by simp
  then show ?case using run_crash.IH c by simp
qed

section \<open>The witness runs: one protocol, three crash placements\<close>

text \<open>
  The protocol chain reuses the banked cyclic witness states
  @{const W0} to @{const W5} as bases; two states are new: the
  post-second-delivery running state (@{text dp_run_w}: both events
  committed, enqueued and delivered, ledger of two justified emissions,
  epoch 0) and its crash (@{text dp_crash_w}).  The wrapper chain
  @{text pr0_w} to @{text pr8_w} threads the cursor: 0 until the
  first persist, 1 until the second, 2 at completion.
\<close>

definition dp_run_w :: "(nat, nat) dwe_state" where
  "dp_run_w = mkT (mkC [(ec1, e1), (ec2, e2)] [(ec1, e1), (ec2, e2)]
                     [(ec1, e1), (ec2, e2)] {} Running)
                  [(1, 0, ec1, e1), (2, 0, ec2, e2)] 0"

definition dp_crash_w :: "(nat, nat) dwe_state" where
  "dp_crash_w = mkT (mkC [(ec1, e1), (ec2, e2)] [(ec1, e1), (ec2, e2)]
                       [(ec1, e1), (ec2, e2)] {} (Crashed ec2))
                    [(1, 0, ec1, e1), (2, 0, ec2, e2)] 0"

definition pr0_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr0_w = mkW W0 0"
definition pr1_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr1_w = mkW W1 0"
definition pr2_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr2_w = mkW W2 0"
definition pr3_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr3_w = mkW W3 0"
definition pr4_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr4_w = mkW W3 1"
definition pr5_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr5_w = mkW W4 1"
definition pr6_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr6_w = mkW W5 1"
definition pr7_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr7_w = mkW dp_run_w 1"
definition pr8_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where "pr8_w = mkW dp_run_w 2"

text \<open>The three crashed endpoints: the delivered member and the lag
  member of the window pair (both cursor 1 --- the delivered member's
  crash landed inside the second deliver-to-persist window, the lag
  member's before the second delivery), and the post-completion control
  (cursor 2).  The lag member's base is the banked loaded-window crash
  state @{const t_mid_w} itself.\<close>

definition cw_del_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where
  "cw_del_w = mkW dp_crash_w 1"

definition cw_lag_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where
  "cw_lag_w = mkW t_mid_w 1"

definition cw_done_w :: "(nat, nat) dwk_state" where
  "cw_done_w = mkW dp_crash_w 2"

lemmas dwk_state_defs =
  pr0_w_def pr1_w_def pr2_w_def pr3_w_def pr4_w_def pr5_w_def
  pr6_w_def pr7_w_def pr8_w_def
